      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it from a few different angles:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (What it looks and acts like)\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which is uniquely *less dense* than liquid water. Because ice is less dense, it floats (which is why ice cubes float in a drink, and why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing aquatic life to survive winter).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the air as humidity, steam from a boiling kettle, or clouds.\n\n**Other notable properties:**\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water dissolves more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily carry nutrients in our bodies and minerals in the earth.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth (oceans absorb heat) and our own bodies (sweat cools us down).\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—creating surface tension, allowing water bugs to walk on it) and to other surfaces (adhesion—allowing water to travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Ecological Importance (Water on Earth)\n*   **Earth is the \"Blue Planet\":** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**. \n*   **The Distribution:** The vast majority of Earth's water (about 96.5%) is saltwater in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes and rivers.\n*"
